      <div class="accent-line"></div>
      <h2 class="section__title">Equestrian Clothing Manufacturer | Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    </div>
    
    <div style="max-width: 800px;">
      
      <div class="faq-item" role="region">
        <button class="faq-item__question" aria-expanded="false">
          What is the difference between denier and GSM in horse rugs?
          <span class="faq-item__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-item__answer" hidden>
          <p>Denier measures the thickness and strength of the individual threads in the outer fabric | common ratings are 600D, 1200D, and 1680D, with higher numbers meaning a tougher, more tear-resistant weave. GSM (grams per square metre) measures the overall weight of that fabric. Neither one measures warmth. A rug's warmth comes from its fill weight, a separate specification measured in grams of polyester insulation per square metre.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <div class="faq-item" role="region">
        <button class="faq-item__question" aria-expanded="false">
          Does a higher GSM horse rug mean it's warmer?
          <span class="faq-item__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-item__answer" hidden>
          <p>No. GSM measures fabric weight, not insulation. A 300 GSM ripstop shell with no fill is not warm at all, while a rug with 300g of polyester fill is very warm regardless of its shell's GSM. Warmth comes from fill weight (measured separately in grams), not from the outer fabric's GSM. Always check fill weight specifically if warmth is what you're buying for.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <div class="faq-item" role="region">
